Payload conventions (vCard, VIN, SPARQCode, Swiss QR-bill, SEPA/EPC QR, AAMVA)
==========================================================================

This file records where the six payload conventions implemented under
`src/ts/payloads/` come from and the basis on which this project builds
and redistributes helpers for them. None of these are barcode
symbologies of their own -- each is a structured text/data convention
carried as the payload of an existing, already-implemented symbology
(`qr`, `code39` or `pdf417`). Since no new bit-level encoding is
involved, there is no "symbology" patent or specification-copyright
question the way there is for the rest of this project's `licenses/`
inventory; each entry below instead records the payload standard's own
publisher and public-availability basis.

It is an ENGINEERING INVENTORY, NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Items marked [TO VERIFY]
have not been independently confirmed by the author.


vCard (src/ts/payloads/vcard.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
RFC 6350, "vCard Format Specification," IETF, published by the IETF Trust
under IETF's standard royalty-free terms for implementing the protocols
it defines. Publicly available at datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6350.
Field structure, escaping rules (§3.4) and the VERSION 3.0 dialect (the
one most broadly recognized by QR-reading phone camera apps) were read
directly from the RFC. No proprietary "smart QR" product's conventions
are involved.


VIN -- Vehicle Identification Number (src/ts/payloads/vin.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ISO 3779 defines the 17-character structure; the check-digit algorithm
implemented here (position 9, transliteration table, weighted sum mod 11)
is the North American scheme mandated by US federal regulation, 49 CFR
571.115 (FMVSS 115) / SAE J853 -- a US government regulation and an SAE
standard, both publicly describable; the transliteration table and
weights are widely published (e.g. in NHTSA's own VIN decoder
documentation) and were verified against a known-good reference VIN
during testing. Not every VIN-issuing jurisdiction outside North America
requires this exact check digit; `validateVIN` reports agreement with
this specific scheme, not universal VIN validity. Carried on a Code 39
label, this project's own already-implemented, publicly-specified
(ISO/IEC 16388) symbology -- no separate barcode symbology work involved.


SPARQCode conventions (src/ts/payloads/sparqcode.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
See `docs/formats/qr-family.md`: "SPARQCode" (MSKYNET, Inc.,
~2010, later Yahoo/Verizon-linked) was found to name a curated set of
already-public, non-proprietary conventions -- `mailto:`/`tel:`/`sms:`
URI schemes, RFC 5870 `geo:`, the widely-deployed `WIFI:` configuration
string, RFC 6350 vCard, and plain URLs -- not a bit-level format MSKYNET
invented. This module implements those same public conventions directly
from their own specifications, not from any MSKYNET material. Wikipedia's
own "SPARQCode" article states MSKYNET released the name itself "free of
any license"; this project's use of the name is descriptive (identifying
which curated convention set is being reproduced), not a product-branding
claim, mirroring the reasoning in `licenses/kartrak-aci.license` for
historical product names used descriptively.


Swiss QR-bill (src/ts/payloads/swiss-qr.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
"Swiss Implementation Guidelines for the QR-bill," Version 2.3
(20.11.2023), published by SIX Interbank Clearing Ltd and freely
downloadable from six-group.com for implementing the standard --
six-group.com/dam/download/banking-services/standardization/qr-bill/
ig-qr-bill-v2.3-en.pdf. Read directly from that PDF: the fixed
4.2.2 data-element structure (§4.2.2 -- including that v2.3 removed the
"combined address" option, so only structured addresses are
implemented), the §4.1.4 separator/omission rule, and the Annex B
"Modulo 10 recursive" QR-reference check-digit algorithm. The Annex B
table itself is rendered as a diagram image in the PDF, not extractable
as text; the table and algorithm implemented here were independently
verified by reproducing the document's own Annex B worked example
byte-for-byte (input `21 00000 00003 13947 14300 0901` must produce
check digit `7`) before being accepted. IBAN/QR-IBAN validation uses the
public ISO 13616 MOD-97-10 algorithm (shared with every IBAN, not
Swiss-specific) and the publicly documented QR-IBAN IID range
(30000-31999). This is a national banking-industry data-interchange
format, not a barcode symbology; it is not a DENSO/Fraunhofer/AIM-style
symbology-copyright case.


SEPA / EPC QR Code (src/ts/payloads/sepa-qr.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
EPC069-12, "Quick Response Code -- Guidelines to Enable the Data Capture
for the Initiation of an SCT," Version 3.1 (19 March 2024), published by
the European Payments Council and freely downloadable from
europeanpaymentscouncil.eu for implementing the standard. Read directly
from that PDF: the ordered field list (§2.2), the line-separator and
trailing-empty-field-omission rule (verified against both of the
document's own worked examples by counting line feeds), the BIC
mandatory-in-v001/optional-in-v002 distinction (tied by the document's
own revision history to EU Regulation 260/2012, Article 5(7)), and the
331-byte/QR-version-13/ECC-level-M payload cap. Not a barcode symbology;
a European banking-industry payment-initiation data format.


AAMVA DL/ID Card Design Standard (src/ts/payloads/aamva.ts)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
"AAMVA DL/ID Card Design Standard," 2020 edition (the standard's own bar
codes are designated AAMVA Version "10"), published by the American
Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators and freely downloadable from
aamva.org for implementing the standard. Read directly from that PDF:
the fixed header structure (D.12.3: Compliance Indicator `@`, Data
Element Separator LF, Record Separator RS, Segment Terminator CR, File
Type `ANSI `, IIN, AAMVA/jurisdiction version, entry count), the Subfile
Designator format (D.12.4: type, offset, length), and the Table D.3
mandatory data elements (the 22 fields this module builds). The header
and offset/length computation were independently verified against the
document's own D.13 worked example (a Virginia sample: DL subfile at
offset 41 length 278, second subfile at offset 319 = 41 + 278).

This module only produces the structured *data* format defined by an
openly published industry standard -- the same kind of structured-text
builder several existing open-source PDF417 tools already provide for
legitimate uses (an issuing authority's own card-personalization
pipeline, or a business testing its own age-verification/ID scanner
against the standard). It carries no jurisdiction's real Issuer
Identification Number by default -- `iin` is a required field a caller
must supply, never defaulted to any specific real jurisdiction -- and
produces only a data structure, nothing resembling a physical card,
security laminate, or any other feature of an actual issued credential.


AADHAAR: deliberately not implemented
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
India's Aadhaar Secure QR Code was researched for the same "payload
convention" treatment as the six formats above and found NOT to fit it,
for a reason distinct from every exclusion in this project's main
`LICENSE` §6 inventory: a "valid," verifiable Aadhaar QR code is
cryptographically signed with a private key held exclusively by UIDAI
(India's Unique Identification Authority), the sole legitimate issuer.
No third party can produce a code that verifies as authentic without
that key, unlike every format in this file (and every symbology this
project implements), which are freely constructible by any implementer
from a public specification. A structured-data builder that reproduces
only the unsigned data layout would be functionally inert for the
format's actual purpose while looking like a genuine government identity
credential -- a materially different, and materially worse, risk profile
than AAMVA's openly-specified, freely-issuable driver's-license data
format. See `docs/guides/legal-exclusions.md` for the full reasoning.


This is an engineering assessment, not legal advice. Items marked
[TO VERIFY] have not been independently confirmed by the author. If your
use is commercially sensitive, obtain your own legal review.
